; ---- frame rates ----
; PERIODS in ms, so smaller is faster and more expensive. These were 8 and 16 -
; 125 fps busy, 62 fps idle - which is the single largest CPU cost in the suite
; and buys nothing: every surface here is composited in software by GDI+ onto a
; layered window, and no display shows more than its refresh rate anyway. The
; overlay's own code already described the intent as "60 fps when armed/hovered,
; 30 fps dormant baseline"; the constants had simply drifted away from it.
; 16/33 IS that intent, and roughly halves both render loops on its own.
;
; TICK_Z is a third tier the hub drops to once nothing has animated and the
; pointer has been away from it for a few seconds. All that is still moving then
; is ambient decoration - slow sine breathing, a travelling sheen - and 20 fps
; carries that. Any pointer contact puts it straight back on TICK_A, so it is
; never the rate you are looking at while you are actually using the thing.
global TOG_MS := 650, TICK_A := 16, TICK_S := 33, TICK_D := 33, TICK_Z := 50
; ---- TICK_G: the in-game stand-down ----
; The one CPU cost in this whole suite that is provably taken from Roblox is
; this suite. Every surface here is composited in SOFTWARE by GDI+ onto a
; layered window and handed to DWM - the hub alone is an 832 x 572 target being
; fully redrawn, and TICK_S is thirty of those a second. It runs whether or not
; you can see it: HubTim only stops for hubHidden, not for "the game is in
; front", so a hub sitting behind full-screen Roblox has been drawing at thirty
; frames a second into a window nobody is looking at.
;
; Two frames a second while the game has focus. Nothing is lost - you are not
; looking at it, and the moment the pointer touches it or you tab back, `busy`
; and the poke path put it straight back on the fast tier. Applied only while
; FPS BOOST is on, so it arrives with the feature it belongs to rather than
; changing the hub's behaviour behind your back.
;
; ---- 100, not 500, and only from deep idle ----
; It was 500 ms - two frames a second - and it was taken the moment nothing was
; animating, which with the pointer parked on a blank part of the card is most
; of the time. The hub is ALWAYS-ON-TOP over the game; somebody playing with it
; open in the corner was looking at ambient motion drawn at 2 fps, which reads
; as the hub having broken, and a tab change or a minimise put it right for the
; length of their animations and then it "broke" again. The deep-idle tier
; already knows when the hub is genuinely being left alone (four seconds with
; no pointer contact, HUB_DEEP_MS), and its own note says slow sine motion
; needs 20 fps to carry. So the stand-down starts where deep idle starts, not
; before, and it halves the deep rate rather than dividing it by ten: ten
; frames a second is the floor for something that is on screen.
global TICK_G := 100
global HUB_DEEP_MS := 4000
