; ---- how big the interface is drawn ----
; Every surface in this suite is laid out in BASE UNITS and presented through a
; world transform, so one factor here sizes all of them - the hub, the overlay,
; the loading screen, the gate, the gallery, the cropper, the puzzle card, the
; grid setup card and the resolution notice - text and all, because GDI+ renders
; the glyphs through the same transform rather than scaling a bitmap of them.
;
; It used to be DPI ALONE. A_ScreenDPI/96 is 1.0 on every display running at
; 100%, whatever its resolution, so the whole interface was pinned to the pixel
; sizes it was drawn for at 1920x1080: correct there, small on 1440p, half the
; apparent size on 4K, and oversized on anything below. Resolution and DPI are
; different questions and only one of them was being asked.
;
; uiRes answers the other. The reference is the layout's own design resolution,
; and the SHORTER axis wins so a wide display gets a bigger interface only when
; it is genuinely bigger, not merely wider - an ultrawide is not a reason to
; scale up. Clamped at both ends: below 0.72 the small faces stop being legible,
; and above 2.2 the hub would start crowding the screen it is meant to sit
; beside. The PRIMARY screen, not the virtual desktop, because that is what the
; hub centres itself on and what the user is looking at.
;
; NOT applied to anything PUZZLE AI measures. The board, the grid, the markers
; and every injected cursor position are in true screen pixels and must stay
; that way - none of them reads scaleF, and none of them may start.
