; Capture the machine as it is NOW - once, before anything here has touched
; it. This is what a revert puts back, not merely the state one click ago.
DOPBaseEnsure()
; The HKCU baseline is its own file with its own guards, and it must not sit
; behind DOPBaseEnsure's early returns: a machine carrying baseline.json from a
; build before HKCU moved into this process would otherwise never capture one.
DOPUserBaseEnsure()
; An apply whose second half never ran, finished here rather than left half on.
DOPUserReconcile()
; ---- and then keep asking whether it is still true -------------------------
; The apply is a set of persistent writes, so it outlives this process by
; design - that half was never the risk. The risk is the opposite: something
; else on the machine puts one of these values back and the hub goes on showing
; a green row over a default. This is the only thing in the module that looks at
; the machine again after the apply, and it is on a timer rather than on the
; panel's paint so it keeps answering whether or not DEVICE OPTIMIZATIONS - or
; the hub - is being looked at.
